---
import TextPage from '../layouts/TextPage.astro';
import { CONTACT } from '../lib/site';
---

<TextPage
  title="Contact RosterOwl — bug reports, requests, questions | RosterOwl"
  description="Get in touch with the people who build RosterOwl. Bug reports, feature requests, district privacy questions — email a human, no ticket system."
  heading="Get in touch"
  lede="There's no support portal and no chatbot — <strong>email reaches a person</strong> who works on the tools. Bug reports and feature requests are read, and the annoying ones tend to get fixed first."
>
  <h2>Email us</h2>
  <p class="mail" data-user={CONTACT.user} data-domain={CONTACT.domain}>
    {CONTACT.user} <span aria-hidden="true">[at]</span><span class="sr-only">@</span>
    {CONTACT.domain}
  </p>
  <p>
    We aim to reply within a couple of business days. During the first week of a school year, make
    that a bit longer — we know why you're writing then, and so is everyone else.
  </p>

  <h2>Reporting a bug</h2>
  <p>
    A tool that misbehaves is worth telling us about even if you found a way around it. The things
    that help most:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Which tool, and what you were doing when it went wrong.</li>
    <li>What you expected to happen, and what happened instead.</li>
    <li>Your browser and device — "Safari on an iPad", "Chrome on a school laptop".</li>
    <li>If it's about a PDF, the PDF itself, which you can attach.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Please don't send us your class list.</strong> We never receive student names by
      design (<a href="/privacy/">here's how that works</a>), and emailing them to us would be the
      one way to break that. A description, or a screenshot with the names blurred, is plenty.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Feature requests</h2>
  <p>
    The filter we apply is simple: <em>does the stored class list make this better?</em> Another way
    to use your roster is very likely a yes. A gradebook, a parent-messaging system, or behavior
    tracking is a no — not because they're bad ideas, but because they're a different product and
    we'd do them badly.
  </p>

  <h2>Schools and districts</h2>
  <p>
    If you need a vendor privacy questionnaire completed, a data-privacy agreement reviewed, or a
    written statement that no student data leaves the device, email us and say which form you need.
    <a href="/about/#student-data">The short version is on the about page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Something's gone wrong with my saved classes</h2>
  <p>
    Because your data lives in your browser rather than on our server, we can't look it up or
    restore it for you — and we can't accidentally lose it either. If a class has vanished, try the
    same browser and device you set it up in, and check the version history inside the tool before
    doing anything else. Then email us: knowing how it happened is how we stop it happening to
    someone else.
  </p>
</TextPage>

<script is:inline>
  // No mailto: and no full address in the served HTML — the two halves are only
  // joined here, at runtime. Without JS the page still shows a readable
  // "hello [at] rosterowl.com", so nobody is left without a way to reach us.
  (() => {
    const host = document.querySelector('p.mail');
    if (!host) return;
    const { user, domain } = host.dataset;
    const link = document.createElement('a');
    link.href = `mailto:${user}@${domain}`;
    link.textContent = `${user}@${domain}`;
    host.replaceChildren(link);
  })();
</script>

<style>
  .mail,
  .mail :global(a) {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--brand);
  }
</style>
